course: dictionary
lesson: 13538
title: Importation: goods from abroad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Importation is a noun. It comes from the verb import, which means to bring goods into one country from another. The ending -ation turns the action into a thing: the act of bringing something in. «The importation of tea and spices made the market busy with new smells.» Tea and spices came from other countries, so importation must mean bringing them in.
explain: The word has a second meaning too: something brought in from somewhere else, such as a plant, an animal or a custom. «The importation of rabbits into Australia caused big problems for farmers.» Here importation means the rabbits themselves, which were brought there from another place.
explain: Look at the words around importation to find its meaning. «The government will ban the importation of weapons.» Ban means stop by law, so the law stops weapons being brought into the country. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «the government will stop bringing weapons in from abroad» still make sense? Then it is right.
fact: Importation means bringing goods or things into a country from another country.
fact: Importation comes from the verb import, and the ending -ation shows the action or its result.
fact: «Importation» of tea, spices, cotton or grain shows trade between countries.
fact: Laws control the importation of animals, plants and weapons, so some things need permission before they can come in.
fact: Some importations, like rabbits in Australia or cane toads, caused big problems because they spread and harmed other living things.
exercise: «The importation of tea and spices brought new flavours to the town.» What does importation mean here? | Making tea at home | *Bringing goods into a country from another country | Sending letters to a friend abroad | Taking goods out of a country to sell
exercise: «The rabbits were an importation from Europe, and they spread fast.» In this sentence, what is an importation? | A sunny day on the farm | The farm fence | The map of Europe | *The rabbits, brought in from another place
exercise: «The law will ban the importation of weapons.» What does the law stop? | Mending broken weapons | Selling old weapons in a museum | Making weapons in the country | *Bringing weapons into the country
test: «Laws control the importation of animals and plants.» Which word tells you that rules are used to manage importation? | Animals | Plants | Importation | *Laws
test: Which of these is an example of an importation? | A child reading a book at home | *Tea brought from India to sell in our shops | A bus driving through town | A farmer growing wheat in his own field
exam: «The importation of cane toads caused big problems in Australia.» What is the best meaning of importation here? | A petrol station | *Something brought in from another place, like cane toads | Living in the bush | A grey, wet day
